Z||000005||batboy||02-19-2000||08:50 PM||||Replacing a head gasket is not too hard. Well, it's not real easy either, although mainly time consuming. It has to be done right. Often, if the head gasket fails, then the head surface is warped too, which means a trip to the machine shop so they can mill the head. Has the engine ever ran hot? This is the number one reason for blowing a head gasket. Are you sure it's a leaking head gasket? Have you done a compression check? I'd use a Fel-Pro gasket and the head bolts need to be torque to the right specs and tightened up using the proper torquing sequence. Good luck. By the way, when I get a different set of wheels for my Batmobile (soon I hope), I'll sell you my old 14" Fiero factory alloy wheels really, really, really cheap. I have plenty of pics of those wheels on my website.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Lance||02-19-2000||09:58 PM||||Yea batboy, That sounds great. Let me know when you get those new rims and I'll buy those you have on now from ya.<P>Thanx||||reg||6
Z||000007||Oreif||02-19-2000||10:32 PM||||Lance, <BR>Very Nice! If you plan on waiting for the rims for a while I think I might have the plastic wheel centers for the ones missing. I'm not sure but I think I have 2 or 3. They were off of a friends 85. (I sold him my old 14" rims, and use his old 13" for when the car is stored during the winter) I might even have a chrome ring or 2 I'll have to look tomorrow in the garage to be sure if your interested.||||reg||1
